UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyA$# Certificate of Compliance (CoC): what it is and when you actually need one

**Short answer:** A Certificate of Compliance is a document, usually issued by the manufacturer or an accredited lab, stating that a specific product or batch meets identified standards or regulatory requirements. It is evidence, not a licence: its value depends entirely on who issued it, what was tested, and whether the underlying requirements are correctly identified. Marketplaces, customs authorities and B2B buyers ask for CoCs as a quick trust signal before they dig into full technical documentation.

## Key facts

- A CoC certifies that a defined product (model, batch or serial range) conforms to named standards or regulations, based on stated evidence.
- It is evidence, not a licence: a CoC that names no standard, cites no test report and covers no defined product scope certifies nothing.
- A test report records what a lab measured; a declaration of conformity is the manufacturer's formal legal statement; a CoC sits between them as a concise attestation.
- Common triggers include marketplace compliance reviews, B2B buyer qualification, customs holds, and tenders requiring a compliance file per SKU.
- A credible CoC names the issuer, the exact product scope, the standards with versions and dates, the test reports relied upon, the issue date, and a signatory.
- In the US, children's products subject to CPSC rules need a Children's Product Certificate based on third-party testing; general-use products need a General Certificate of Conformity.
- Under the CPSC eFiling rule, importers must electronically file certificate data at the time of entry for regulated products.

## 1. What a CoC actually certifies

A Certificate of Compliance is a written attestation that a defined product conforms to identified requirements. The three load-bearing elements are the product scope (which model, which batch, which serial range), the requirements (which standards or regulations, with versions and dates), and the evidence (which test reports, inspections, or assessments support the claim). Remove any one of them and the document stops being useful: a CoC for "all our products" against "all applicable standards" with no test references is a decoration, not evidence.

Understand what a CoC does not do. It does not replace the underlying test reports or the technical documentation. It does not create compliance where the testing was inadequate or the wrong standard was applied. And it does not transfer legal responsibility: the party placing the product on the market remains responsible for compliance regardless of which certificates it holds. Treat the CoC as the cover page of the evidence file, and build the file first.

## 2. CoC vs test report vs declaration of conformity

These three documents are constantly confused, and the confusion causes real commercial damage. A test report records what a laboratory measured on a specific sample, under specific conditions, against a specific standard. It says what happened in the lab; it does not say the product is compliant in any general sense.

A declaration of conformity, such as the EU Declaration of Conformity, is the manufacturer's formal legal statement that the product complies with specific legislation. It carries legal weight because the signatory takes legal responsibility for the statement. In the EU system, drawing up the declaration is a defined step in placing a CE-marked product on the market.

A Certificate of Compliance sits between them: a concise attestation, often one page, that references the fuller evidence. Regulators want the declaration and the test reports; buyers, marketplaces and customs brokers often accept a CoC as the summary page that tells them the full file exists and what it contains. When a marketplace asks for "certification", it usually means something in this family, and the fastest resolution is to ask which document, for which standard, covering which SKU, then supply exactly that.

## 3. When you will be asked for one

Marketplace compliance reviews are the most common trigger. Amazon and other marketplaces gate certain categories, children's products, electronics, cosmetics, personal protective equipment, on documentation, and a listing can be suspended until the documents are supplied. B2B buyers qualifying a new supplier routinely ask for CoCs per SKU as part of vendor onboarding. Customs authorities, when they hold a shipment for a compliance check, want quick evidence of conformity before they dig into the full file. Tenders and retail listings frequently require a compliance file per SKU, with the CoC as its first page.

The pattern is consistent: the CoC is asked for at a gate, and the gate does not wait. Keep CoCs ready before you list, before you ship, and before the buyer asks, because producing one under a 48-hour marketplace deadline from a standing start is how errors happen.

## 4. What a credible CoC contains

Compliance teams check the same fields every time. A credible CoC contains: the issuer's identity and, where relevant, accreditation (name, address, accreditation body and scope); the exact product identification (model numbers, batch or lot numbers, serial ranges, and the configuration covered); the standards or regulations claimed, with their full designations, version years and dates; the test report numbers and dates it relies upon, with the issuing laboratory identified; the date of issue; the period or production scope the certificate covers; and a named signatory with their role.

Vague CoCs with no standard references, no product identification, and no evidence trail are routinely rejected by compliance teams, and rightly so: they provide no basis for a decision. If you issue CoCs to your customers, include every field above; the ten extra minutes per document save weeks of back-and-forth later.

## 5. Red flags in CoCs you receive from suppliers

Audit supplier CoCs the way a marketplace compliance team would. Watch for certificates issued by the supplier itself with no independent testing behind them; standards cited without version years (so you cannot tell which edition was applied); product scope described as "all our products" or a brand name with no model or batch identification; expiry or validity dates far in the future for standards that are regularly revised; CoCs that arrive suspiciously fast for complex products; test report numbers that cannot be verified with the issuing lab; and issuing laboratories you cannot find in accreditation databases for the cited scope.

When a red flag appears, go back to the source: ask for the underlying test reports, verify the lab's accreditation for the specific standard and scope, and check that the report's product description matches what you are actually buying. A supplier who cannot produce the underlying evidence does not have a compliance file; they have a template.

## 6. The US system: CPC, GCC, and eFiling

In the United States, the Consumer Product Safety Act framework makes certificates a legal duty, not just a commercial convenience. Children's products subject to a CPSC rule, ban, standard or regulation require a Children's Product Certificate (CPC), based on testing by a CPSC-accepted third-party laboratory, and the certificate must accompany the product or shipment. General-use (non-children's) products subject to CPSC rules require a General Certificate of Conformity (GCC), which may be based on the manufacturer's own testing or a reasonable testing programme.

Under the CPSC's eFiling final rule (published 8 January 2025, amending 16 CFR Part 1110), importers of consumer products subject to a CPSC rule, ban, standard or regulation must electronically file certificate data at the time of entry through CBP's ACE system, rather than producing certificates only when asked. Filing becomes mandatory for most imports from 8 July 2026, and for goods withdrawn from Foreign Trade Zones from 8 January 2027. Importers can file full data per entry, use disclaimer codes where flagged HTS codes need no certificate, or pre-register certificates in the CPSC Product Registry and reference them per entry. Map your HTS codes to CPSC rules now, and confirm certificate data exists for every regulated SKU before the deadline.

| Document | Issued by | Based on | Legal weight | Asked for by |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Test report | Laboratory | Measurements on a sample | Factual record | Regulators, buyers verifying claims |
| Certificate of Compliance | Manufacturer or lab | Test reports, inspections | Attestation; value depends on issuer and evidence | Marketplaces, buyers, customs, tenders |
| EU Declaration of Conformity | Manufacturer | Technical documentation | Formal legal statement under EU law | Market surveillance authorities |
| Children's Product Certificate (US) | Manufacturer/importer | CPSC-accepted third-party lab testing | Statutory requirement for children's products | CPSC, CBP, retailers |
| General Certificate of Conformity (US) | Manufacturer/importer | Testing or reasonable testing programme | Statutory requirement for regulated general-use products | CPSC, CBP, retailers |

## Frequently asked questions

### Is a CoC legally required?

It depends on the market and the product. In the EU, the legally required document for CE-marked products is the EU Declaration of Conformity; a CoC is usually a commercial requirement from buyers or marketplaces. In the US, CPCs and GCCs are statutory requirements for products subject to CPSC rules, and eFiling makes the certificate data a condition of smooth import.

### Can I issue my own CoC?

Manufacturers routinely issue their own CoCs, and there is nothing inherently wrong with it. Its credibility rests entirely on the underlying test evidence: a self-issued CoC backed by accredited test reports for the stated product scope is useful; a self-issued CoC backed by nothing is not.

### How long is a CoC valid?

Until the product, the standards, or the evidence change; there is no universal expiry date. A CoC tied to a specific batch covers that batch. A CoC referencing a standard that has since been revised needs review. Date the certificate, scope it precisely, and re-issue when anything material changes.

### What should I do when a marketplace suspends my listing over documentation?

Read the suspension notice carefully to identify exactly which document, standard and SKU is at issue. Supply precisely that, with the full evidence chain behind it: the CoC as the summary page, the test reports it cites, and the declaration where applicable. Do not send a generic company certificate and hope it passes.

### How do I verify a supplier's CoC?

Check the issuer's identity and accreditation for the cited scope, confirm the test report numbers with the issuing laboratory, verify the product description matches your purchase, and confirm the standard version cited is current. If any link in the chain fails, treat the CoC as unverified and require fresh evidence.

### Does a CoC replace the technical file?

No. The CoC is the cover page; the technical file (test reports, declarations, design records, traceability) is the evidence. Regulators ask for the file. Keep both, and keep them consistent with each other.

## Official sources

- [EU Blue Guide: conformity assessment and documentation duties](https://single-market-economy.ec.europa.eu/news/blue-guide-implementation-product-rules-2022-published-2022-06-29_en)
- [16 CFR Part 1110, Certificates of Compliance (govinfo)](https://www.govinfo.gov/app/details/CFR-2024-title16-vol2/part1110)
- [CPSC eFiling final rule, full text (Federal Register)](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2025/01/08/2024-30826.txt)

## Last verified

September 2026. Requirements differ by product category and market; this is general process guidance. Children's products and other high-risk categories carry additional certification duties beyond a basic CoC.

Ready? [Check your product's requirements](/tools/can-i-sell-this).$thickbodyA$, sections = '[{"heading": "What a CoC certifies", "summary": "Scope, requirements, and evidence; evidence not licence."}, {"heading": "CoC versus test report versus declaration", "summary": "Where the CoC sits in the evidence stack."}, {"heading": "When you are asked for one", "summary": "Marketplaces, buyers, customs, and tenders."}, {"heading": "Anatomy of a credible CoC", "summary": "The fields compliance teams check."}, {"heading": "Supplier CoC red flags", "summary": "How to spot and verify weak certificates."}, {"heading": "US CPC, GCC, and eFiling", "summary": "Statutory certificates and electronic filing."}]'::jsonb, short_answer = 'A Certificate of Compliance is a document, usually from the manufacturer or an accredited lab, stating that a defined product or batch meets named standards or regulations. It is evidence, not a licence: its value depends on the issuer, the testing behind it, and correctly identified requirements. Marketplaces, buyers, customs and tenders ask for CoCs as a trust signal.', updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'certificate-of-compliance-guide';

UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyA$# CPSC eFiling: electronic certificates of compliance

**Short answer:** Under the CPSC eFiling final rule published 8 January 2025 (90 Fed. Reg. 1800), which amends 16 CFR Part 1110, importers of consumer products subject to a CPSC rule, ban, standard or regulation must electronically file certificate data at the time of entry. Filing is mandatory for most imports from 8 July 2026, and for goods withdrawn from Foreign Trade Zones from 8 January 2027.

## Key facts

- The final rule published 8 January 2025 (90 Fed. Reg. 1800) amends 16 CFR Part 1110, moving from certificates produced on request to certificate data filed at entry.
- It covers importers of finished consumer products subject to a CPSC rule, ban, standard or regulation, including Children's Product Certificates (CPC) and General Certificates of Conformity (GCC).
- It applies at every shipment value, including low-value entries that previously received informal low-documentation treatment.
- The PGA message set in CBP's ACE system carries product identification, the certifying party, each safety rule certified to, manufacture and test details, and the test-record custodian's contact details.
- Importers can file full data per entry, use disclaimer codes where a flagged HTS code needs no certificate, or pre-register certificates in the CPSC Product Registry and reference them per entry.
- Mandatory for most imports from 8 July 2026; for goods withdrawn from Foreign Trade Zones for consumption or warehousing, from 8 January 2027.
- The rule changes how certificate data is managed, not which products require a certificate.

## 1. What changed: from on-request to at-entry

Since 2008, importers of consumer products subject to CPSC safety rules have been required to maintain Certificates of Compliance, but those certificates only had to be produced when CPSC or CBP asked for them. In practice, that meant certificate data lived in filing cabinets and inboxes, inconsistent in format and often incomplete, and enforcement depended on physical examination or post-entry audit.

The eFiling final rule changes the timing and the medium. Importers must now transmit certificate data electronically into CBP's Automated Commercial Environment (ACE) at the time of entry, using the CPSC Partner Government Agency (PGA) message set. CBP shares the data with CPSC, which uses it to target high-risk shipments, verify compliance before release, and build enforcement intelligence. The policy logic is straightforward: structured data at entry lets the agencies screen every regulated shipment, not just the ones they physically examine.

For importers, the practical shift is from document storage to data management. The certificate still exists as a legal attestation, but its data elements must now be systematised, validated, and transmittable per entry line.

## 2. Who must file

The duty falls on the importer of record for finished consumer products subject to a mandatory CPSC safety rule. That includes children's products requiring a Children's Product Certificate (based on third-party testing by a CPSC-accepted laboratory) and general-use products requiring a General Certificate of Conformity. Private labellers and importers who specify the product are importers for this purpose; the duty follows the import transaction, not the brand name on the box.

Two scope points deserve attention. First, the rule applies at every shipment value, including entries that previously used informal low-documentation treatment and de minimis shipments. Low value does not mean low risk in the agencies' view, and small-parcel e-commerce is explicitly within the enforcement logic. Second, different lines in a single entry may use different filing methods: one line can carry full data, another can reference the Product Registry, and a third can carry a disclaimer code. Design the process line by line, not entry by entry.

## 3. What data is filed: the PGA message set

The PGA message set in ACE carries a defined set of certificate data elements for each regulated product line: product identification (sufficient to identify the finished product), the certifying party (the domestic manufacturer or importer certifying compliance), each CPSC safety rule, ban, standard or regulation certified to, the date and place of manufacture, the date and place of testing, and contact details for the custodian of the test records.

Each element has an operational implication. Product identification must tie the filing to the actual goods in the shipment; vague descriptions create mismatch risk. The safety rules cited must be the rules that actually apply to the product; citing the wrong rule is worse than citing none, because it signals the compliance analysis was not done. Manufacture and test dates and places must be real and current; stale test data attached to a new production run will not survive scrutiny. And the custodian contact must reach a person who can actually produce the test records, because the filing invites the follow-up request.

## 4. Filing options: full filing, disclaimers, and the Product Registry

Importers have three methods, usable in combination within a single entry. Full filing transmits the complete certificate data set in the PGA message set for the entry line; it is the default and the most transparent. Disclaimer codes are used where an HTS code flagged for CPSC review does not in fact require a certificate for the specific goods (for example, a product class that includes both regulated and unregulated items); the disclaimer tells the system why no certificate data accompanies the line. The CPSC Product Registry allows importers to pre-register certificate data and then file only a reference identifier per entry, which suits high-volume importers with stable product lines.

Choose the method per product line based on stability and volume: registry references for steady, high-volume SKUs; full filing for new, changed, or low-volume products where the data is freshly assembled; disclaimers only where genuinely applicable, with the analysis documented, because systematic disclaimer use on regulated goods is an enforcement signal.

## 5. Deadlines and the Foreign Trade Zone track

Filing is mandatory for most imports from 8 July 2026. For goods withdrawn from a Foreign Trade Zone for consumption or warehousing, the mandatory date is 8 January 2027, reflecting the different entry mechanics of FTZ withdrawals. Between the rule's publication and the mandatory dates, the programme has operated on a voluntary basis, with importer participation, system testing, and operational guidance releases.

Treat the mandatory dates as the date by which the full process must be working, not the date to start building it. The work includes mapping HTS codes to CPSC rules, confirming certificate data exists for every regulated SKU, registering with the Product Registry where that method will be used, configuring the ACE message set with the customs broker or software provider, and running test filings. Each of those steps has its own lead time, and the broker's readiness is not your readiness until you have tested your own data through the chain.

## 6. Preparation checklist for importers

Work through this sequence. First, inventory every imported SKU and map its HTS classification to the CPSC rules, bans, standards and regulations that apply; record the analysis, including why a flagged HTS code needs a disclaimer where applicable. Second, confirm that a current certificate exists for every regulated SKU, with the underlying test reports from the appropriate laboratory (CPSC-accepted third-party lab for children's products). Third, systematise the certificate data elements so they can be produced per shipment without manual reassembly: product identification, certifying party, rules cited, manufacture and test details, custodian contact. Fourth, decide the filing method per product line and register with the Product Registry where references will be used. Fifth, configure the PGA message set with the customs broker or ACE software provider and run test filings well before the mandatory date. Sixth, assign ownership: a named person responsible for certificate data quality, broker coordination, and CPSC correspondence.

| Element | Full filing | Product Registry reference | Disclaimer |
|---|---|---|---|
| Data transmitted per entry | Complete certificate data set | Reference identifier only | Disclaimer code with reason |
| Best for | New, changed, or low-volume products | Stable, high-volume SKUs | Flagged HTS codes with no certificate requirement |
| Setup effort | Per-entry data assembly | Upfront registration; light per-entry | Documented applicability analysis |
| Risk if misused | Data errors per entry | Stale registry data | Enforcement signal if overused |

## Frequently asked questions

### Does eFiling change which products need a certificate?

No. The rule changes how certificate data is managed (filed electronically at entry) not which products require a certificate. Any consumer product subject to a CPSC rule, ban, standard or regulation continues to require a Certificate of Compliance; eFiling is the new transmission mechanism for its data.

### Do low-value and de minimis shipments have to eFile?

Yes. The rule applies at every shipment value, including entries that previously used informal low-documentation treatment. Build the process for small parcels with the same rigour as for container loads.

### What is the CPSC Product Registry?

A system in which importers pre-register certificate data and then reference it by identifier in the per-entry PGA message set, instead of transmitting the full data set each time. It suits importers with stable, high-volume product lines and reduces per-entry data assembly.

### When should we start testing with our broker?

As soon as the certificate data is systematised. The mandatory dates (8 July 2026 generally; 8 January 2027 for FTZ withdrawals) are the dates the process must be working, and broker configuration, message-set validation, and data quality fixes all take time. Test filings during the voluntary period are the low-risk way to find problems.

### What happens if we fail to eFile after the mandatory date?

Expect shipment delays, increased examination and targeting, and enforcement exposure. CPSC has indicated it will use the submitted data to adjust risk scoring; missing or poor data marks the shipment as higher risk. The commercial cost of border delays on a just-in-time supply chain usually exceeds the cost of building the filing process properly.

### Who is the importer for eFiling purposes when we use a customs broker?

The importer of record remains responsible for the certificate and its data. The broker transmits the PGA message set as your agent, but the duty, and the liability for inaccurate data, sits with the importer. Oversee the broker's work; do not outsource the responsibility.

## Official sources

- [CPSC eFiling final rule, full text (Federal Register, 8 January 2025)](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2025/01/08/2024-30826.txt)
- [16 CFR Part 1110, Certificates of Compliance (govinfo)](https://www.govinfo.gov/app/details/CFR-2024-title16-vol2/part1110)

